    <h1 class="fade-up">Study garden design for a <em>calm place to work</em></h1>
    <p class="lead fade-up d2">Study garden design for garden offices, writing rooms and home studies: calm planting, framed views and quiet paths that make working from home a pleasure.</p>
    <div class="hero__actions fade-up d3"><a class="btn" href="/contact.html">Discuss your garden <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div>
  </div></div>
</section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap prose-grid">
  <article class="prose reveal"><p class="lead">More of us now work from home, and many do it in a garden room, a converted outbuilding or a study looking onto the garden. <strong>Study garden design</strong> is about creating the right view and the right walk to work: a garden that helps you concentrate and gives you somewhere to think.</p>
<h2>The view from the desk</h2>
<p>The view from a study window is looked at for hours every day. It should be calm rather than busy: a framed picture of foliage, a tree that moves in the wind, a small area of still water. We plan these views on the drawing board from the position of the desk itself.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use evergreen structure so the view is good in winter, when most work is done.</li>
<li>Choose plants with movement, such as grasses and multi-stemmed birch, for gentle interest.</li>
<li>Keep bright colour and busy areas out of the main sightline.</li>
</ul>
<figure><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/modern-patio.webp" alt="Modern garden room with large windows, chairs and planting outside" width="1050" height="1400" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>A garden room set among planting: the walk to work becomes a walk through the garden.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>The walk to work</h2>
<p>A garden office gives you a commute of twenty metres. Make it count. A stepping-stone path through planting, past a scented shrub by the door (sarcococca in winter, philadelphus in summer), helps separate home from work.</p>
<div class="pull">Twenty metres of garden can be the best commute in London.</div>
<h2>Siting a garden room</h2>
<p>If you are planning a new garden office, where it goes changes the whole garden. We look at light, privacy, the view back to the house and the route for power and data. A well-placed garden room becomes a focal point; a badly placed one dominates the garden. We work with garden-room suppliers and architects to get it right.</p>
<div class="duo"><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/hands-trowel.webp" srcset="/assets/img/hands-trowel-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/hands-trowel.webp 1400w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Gloved hands planting a young plant with a trowel" width="1400" height="927" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/fern-detail.webp" srcset="/assets/img/fern-detail-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/fern-detail.webp 933w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Dense green fern fronds in shade" width="933" height="1400" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><h2>Quiet and screening</h2>
<p>Planting absorbs sound and hides neighbours. A layered boundary of trees, shrubs and climbers is more effective, and far more attractive, than a tall fence. For a study garden this matters as much as anything else.</p></article>
  <aside class="aside">
    <div class="aside-card reveal"><h3>At a glance</h3><dl><div><dt>Typical size</dt><dd>30 to 200 m²</dd></div><div><dt>Best for</dt><dd>Garden offices, studies, writing rooms</dd></div><div><dt>Season of interest</dt><dd>All year</dd></div><div><dt>Signature plants</dt><dd><div class="palette"><span>Betula utilis</span><span>Pittosporum</span><span>Hakonechloa</span><span>Hellebores</span><span>Sarcococca</span><span>Ferns</span></div></dd></div></dl></div>
    <div class="aside-card aside-card--dark reveal"><h3>Planning a study garden?</h3><p>Tell us about your space and we will arrange a consultation visit.</p><a class="btn" href="/contact.html">Get in touch <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><p style="margin:16px 0 0;font-size:.9rem">Or email <a href="mailto:info@fraserandmorris.com" style="color:#fff">info@fraserandmorris.com</a></p></div>
  </aside>
</div></section>
<section class="section section--cream"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head reveal" style="justify-content:center;text-align:center"><div><p class="eyebrow">Questions</p><h2>Study garden FAQs</h2></div></div>
  <div class="faq"><details class="reveal"><summary>Where is the best place for a garden office?</summary><div class="answer"><p>Usually at the end of the garden facing back towards the house, with good light and a view over planting. We assess light, privacy and services before recommending a location.</p></div></details><details class="reveal"><summary>Which plants are best to look at from a study window?</summary><div class="answer"><p>Calm, evergreen structure with some movement: multi-stemmed trees, grasses such as hakonechloa, ferns and clipped shapes, with seasonal highlights from bulbs and hellebores.</p></div></details><details class="reveal"><summary>Do I need planning permission for a garden room?</summary><div class="answer"><p>Many garden rooms fall under permitted development, but rules on size, height and use apply. We always recommend checking with your local authority or architect.</p></div></details></div>
</div></section>
